(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-vignettes"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 9-year-old presents with a sudden shower of small raindrop-shaped scaly pink papules over the trunk and proximal limbs. He had a sore throat about two weeks ago. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Guttate psoriasis","Pityriasis rosea","Plaque psoriasis","Nummular eczema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — raindrop-shaped papules in a child 2 to 3 weeks after streptococcal infection or an upper respiratory infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has progressive recession of the frontotemporal hairline in a triangular pattern with thinning at the crown, developing over several years. His father and grandfather have the same pattern. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Trichotillomania from chronic hair pulling","Androgenetic alopecia, male-pattern hair loss","Alopecia areata","Alopecia totalis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — androgen-dependent, with dihydrotestosterone chiefly responsible and a strong paternal influence on risk. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 146"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman has had several weeks of intense itching with urticarial plaques but no blisters. She is otherwise well. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["The prodromal phase of bullous pemphigoid, which may precede bullae by weeks to months","Chronic idiopathic urticaria entirely unrelated to any blistering disease","Early pemphigus vulgaris","Lichen simplex chronicus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the prodrome presents as pruritic urticarial or oedematous lesions before the blistering phase. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has a glazed, well-demarcated dermatitis over both eyelids that began after she started using a new cosmetic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Perioral dermatitis","Irritant contact dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the eyelids and face are among the commonest sites, from makeup and masks, and the glazed well-demarcated appearance fits. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 9-month-old has a bright red rash in the diaper area. Examination shows beefy erythema involving the inguinal folds with peripheral scale and scattered satellite papules and pustules beyond the main area. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Candidal diaper dermatitis","Irritant diaper dermatitis","Bacterial diaper dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fold involvement with satellite lesions is the candidal pattern; irritant disease spares the folds. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman develops an itchy eruption under a new necklace and beneath the button of her jeans. Which allergen is most likely?","choices":["Nickel","Urushiol","Neomycin","Bacitracin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — nickel is the most common metal allergen, and eruptions reproduce the shape of the item worn. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has burning and small red papules around her mouth. Examination shows grouped monomorphic papules and pustules over the nasolabial folds and chin, sparing a narrow rim next to the vermilion border. She has used hydrocortisone cream daily for several months. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Begin topical adapalene","Apply an occlusive moisturiser several times each day instead","Discontinue the corticosteroid and simplify her skin-care routine","Increase the potency of the topical corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Topical corticosteroid exposure is the most important modifiable association, and continued use perpetuates the condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has a mildly inflamed eczematous patch on the eyelid and another on the vulva. Which topical corticosteroid is most appropriate?","choices":["Clobetasol propionate 0.05%, for a short course","Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% twice daily","Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1% twice daily","Hydrocortisone, or another low potency agent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — potency is chosen by site, and these are the thinnest skin in the body. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has recurrent tapioca-like vesicles on his palms confirmed clinically as dyshidrotic eczema. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral prednisone tapered over three weeks","A high-potency topical corticosteroid","A low-potency topical corticosteroid","A topical antifungal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High potency is specified first line here, with systemic corticosteroids reserved for severe cases. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has several intensely itchy round plaques on his lower legs, 2 to 4 cm across, light pink with scale and crusting. The plaques are uniform throughout without any central clearing. Which is the most appropriate initial diagnostic test?","choices":["Bacterial culture","Allergy patch testing","Punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence","Potassium hydroxide preparation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tinea corporis is the main differential and is excluded by potassium hydroxide, though the absent central clearing already argues against it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with cutaneous lichen planus on the wrists has not improved with emollients. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Topical tacrolimus ointment","Oral prednisone","PUVA phototherapy","Superpotent topical steroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Topical tacrolimus is used for oral and vaginal disease, oral steroids for severe cases, and phototherapy for refractory disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 178"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with recurrent palmar vesicles asks how to reduce flares. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Apply emollients only when the skin feels dry","Avoid irritants such as detergents, solvents and hair dyes, wash with lukewarm water and soap-free cleanser, dry hands thoroughly, and apply emollients immediately afterwards","Wash the hands frequently with an antibacterial soap through the course of the day, in order to prevent secondary bacterial infection of the ruptured vesicles","Use hot water to relieve the itching"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — trigger avoidance and barrier care are the named education points. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 44-year-old with psoriasis has painful, stiff fingers each morning for over an hour, easing with activity. Examination shows a swollen sausage-shaped third digit, and the nails show pitting and separation of the plate from the bed. Which radiographic finding would you expect?","choices":["Pencil-in-a-cup deformity","Bamboo spine","Subchondral sclerosis with marginal osteophytes","Periarticular osteopenia with symmetric erosions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — psoriatic arthritis, associated with HLA-B27, affecting the distal and proximal interphalangeal joints while sparing the metacarpophalangeals. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with deeply pigmented skin has a warm, tender, swollen forearm. The examiner cannot appreciate any erythema. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Conclude that inflammation is absent, since visible erythema is required in order to diagnose it","Perform a Wood's lamp examination to bring out the erythema in a dark room","Take a punch biopsy immediately, since clinical assessment cannot be relied on","Rely on palpation, distribution and secondary change, since erythema is an unreliable signal on darker skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the finding is present but the colour cue is not. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has recurrent scaling of the scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds and external ears. Examination shows greasy, yellow-white scale over mildly erythematous poorly demarcated patches. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Topical mupirocin","Ketoconazole shampoo and topical ketoconazole cream","Oral terbinafine","High-potency topical corticosteroids as monotherapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Topical antifungals are the mainstay because Malassezia overgrowth drives the condition; steroids may be added early to reduce inflammation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 9-year-old with guttate psoriasis following a streptococcal throat infection is otherwise well. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Begin acitretin","Begin a biologic agent such as etanercept, adalimumab, infliximab or ixekizumab","No specific treatment is required, though phototherapy or topical steroids may be used","Begin methotrexate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — guttate psoriasis often needs no treatment. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 164"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man with two patches of alopecia areata on the scalp asks about treatment. Which is the most appropriate first-line option?","choices":["Intralesional corticosteroids","Topical corticosteroids","Topical minoxidil","Oral finasteride"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — first line in adolescents and adults; topical steroids are first line for children aged ten and under. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man develops an itchy violaceous papular eruption on his wrists two months after starting a new antihypertensive. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin superpotent topical steroids without further history","Test for hepatitis C and treat it to clear the rash","Reassure that the eruption is self-limited and requires no action","Review the medication list, since several drugs produce lichenoid reactions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, sulfonamides, tetracyclines, hydrochlorothiazide, quinidine and some beta blockers are named. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 173"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has chronic bilateral lower-leg swelling, itching and skin discoloration. Examination shows poorly demarcated erythematous plaques, pitting oedema and brown hyperpigmentation around both medial ankles. Pedal pulses are diminished. Which is the most appropriate next step before prescribing compression therapy?","choices":["Order patch testing","Begin oral cephalexin","Perform an ankle-brachial index","Obtain a bacterial wound culture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Compression is the cornerstone of management, but only once adequate arterial circulation is established, and the diminished pulses demand that check first. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with perioral dermatitis has stopped her topical corticosteroid. Which is the most appropriate counselling point about what happens next?","choices":["The eruption should clear completely within 48 hours of stopping","She should resume the steroid if the rash worsens at all","The condition will not recur once the corticosteroid has been stopped once","The eruption may temporarily worsen after corticosteroid withdrawal before it improves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — telling her in advance is what stops her restarting the steroid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man with recurrent greasy scaling of the scalp and nasolabial folds asks how long treatment will be needed. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The condition is chronic and relapsing, so repeated and long-term use of medication is often required","A single two-week course of antifungal shampoo is usually curative and needs no repeating","It will resolve permanently once stress is controlled","It is contagious and household members should be treated as well"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that expectation is stated explicitly for this condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 5-year-old with atopic dermatitis has an area of weeping, thickly crusted plaques with pustules over a previously flaring elbow. She is afebrile. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","choices":["Patch testing","Potassium hydroxide preparation","Bacterial culture of the crusted lesions","Herpes simplex virus polymerase chain reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — culture is indicated for purulent, pustular or significantly crusted lesions to rule out infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man reports generalised itching and rough, tight skin each winter, worst on the shins. Examination shows fine scale with a few shallow fissures and scratch marks, without erythematous plaques. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Take short lukewarm showers with a gentle fragrance-free cleanser only where needed, and apply a thick ointment or cream within minutes of bathing","Take long hot showers each morning to hydrate the skin thoroughly, then apply a light lotion once the skin has completely dried","Use an antibacterial soap over the whole body twice daily","Apply a keratolytic containing urea to the fissured areas first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hot water and detergents are among the causes, and prompt moisturising after bathing is the key manoeuvre. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 153"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with widespread flaccid bullae and painful oral erosions has a biopsy showing acantholysis. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Ultrapotent topical steroids alone","Observation, since the disease usually remits in five to six years","Oral doxycycline as monotherapy","Rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone, given urgently"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pemphigus is life-threatening, so treatment is urgent, and a steroid-sparing agent is usually added so steroids can be tapered. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has an oval scaly plaque on his chest that appeared ten days ago, and now smaller oval lesions are spreading down his trunk. Which is the most appropriate initial diagnostic approach?","choices":["Serum rapid plasma reagin","Clinical diagnosis based on the herald patch and the typical pattern","Skin biopsy of the herald patch","Potassium hydroxide preparation taken from the herald patch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the herald patch and cleavage-line distribution are enough. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a purulent wound and the clinician wants to know both what is growing and what will treat it. Which test should be ordered?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide wet preparation","Tzanck smear of the wound base","Bacterial culture and sensitivity","Fungal culture of the wound surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the culture names the organism and the sensitivity directs the antibiotic. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 30-year-old has extensive urushiol contact dermatitis covering both arms, the neck and part of the trunk. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["High-dose oral corticosteroids tapered over two to three weeks","High-potency topical corticosteroids alone","A five-day course of oral prednisone without any taper","Oral antihistamines alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — extensive disease needs systemic therapy, and early discontinuation causes high rates of rebound. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man wishes to treat male-pattern hair loss and asks about finasteride. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It can safely be shared with a female partner who is also experiencing hair thinning at the crown","About 2% of men report reduced libido and erectile function, and these effects reverse when the drug is stopped","The sexual side effects are permanent once they occur","It is most effective at the frontal hairline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It works best combined with minoxidil, and it is not indicated in women and contraindicated in pregnancy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman has bullous pemphigoid limited to a few areas of the trunk. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Rituximab","Low-dose methotrexate with folic acid","Ultrapotent topical steroids","Oral prednisone with azathioprine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — mild disease is managed topically, with oral prednisone or doxycycline reserved for moderate to severe disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 10-month-old has a diaper rash involving the inguinal folds with satellite papules beyond the main area. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","choices":["Patch testing","Skin biopsy","Potassium hydroxide preparation","Bacterial culture and sensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — fold involvement and satellite lesions suggest Candida, and identification of budding yeast or pseudohyphae supports it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 108"}]};
